<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Massana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Massana is one of the seven parishes of Andorra and the name of its main town, in the north-west of the principality on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valira">Valira</a> del Nord. It includes the villages of Arinsal and Pal, whose ski slopes form the Vallnord area, and the Comapedrosa, at 2,942 metres the highest peak of Andorra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-massana.jpg" alt="La Massana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jlmoncada · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LaMassana_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Massana, originally La Maçana, lies in the north-west of the Principality of Andorra, and its name derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> mattianam, a variety of apple. The parish covers 65 square kilometres and adjoins the parishes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordino">Ordino</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encamp">Encamp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/escaldes-engordany">Escaldes-Engordany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andorra-la-vella">Andorra la Vella</a>, and also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its other settlements include Pal, Arinsal, Anyós, Erts, Sispony, L'Aldosa, Escàs, Xixerella, Mas de Ribafeta and Pui. The town of La Massana stands at an average elevation of 1,240 metres.</p><p>The parish is extremely mountainous. Coma Pedrosa, at 2,942 metres the highest point in Andorra, rises in the north-west and has panoramic views of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>; it is the site of the Coma Pedrosa Nature Reserve and usually keeps some snow in summer. The next-highest peaks are Medacorba, Sanfonts, Pla de l'Estany and Racofred. There are four lakes, Forcats, Montmantell, Estany Negre and Les Truites, and three main rivers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valira">Valira</a> del Nord, a 14-kilometre tributary of the Gran Valira which begins at the Llac de Tristaina, the Pal and the Montaner. Above the tree line, at about 2,100 to 2,400 metres, the climate is alpine; the valleys are milder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tourism is the main industry. The ski resort of Vallnord, formed by the Arinsal and Pal sectors, has 89 km of runs between 2,600 and 1,550 metres, and summer visitors hike, climb and cycle in the mountains. The 262 hectares of farmland, 14 per cent of Andorra's agricultural land, grow tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and other vegetables and cereals, and the rough pastures on the higher slopes keep dairy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and horses for trekking.</p><p>There are two museums, the Casa Rull Ethnographic Museum in Sispony and Farga Rossell, a forge built in the mid-19th century that demonstrates iron working. The parish celebrates the festa patronale on 17 November, the festa major on 15 August, the Rosary fete on the second Sunday of July, and a fiesta at the end of June or beginning of July that marks the consecration of the livestock and pastures. A gondola lift links the town to Vallnord throughout the year; there are no railways or airports in Andorra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Massana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local life was run by the parishes, Canillo, Encamp, Ordino, <b>La Massana</b>, Andorra la Vella and Sant Julià de Lòria, with Escaldes-Engordany added as a seventh in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
